! ---------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! FINALIZED (mesh-selection dependent fields only) -- secofs_ufs_ww3 --   !
! ww3_grid.nml (WW3 grid pre-processor namelist, unstructured/UNST mesh). !
! SECOFS wave-mesh preparation (mesh finalization pass, WCOSS2 mesh-prep      !
! task). Selected mesh: the E=5 m elevation-ceiling candidate,            !
! secofs_wave_e5m.msh (1,305,953 nodes / 2,515,436 elements per the       !
! mesh's own $Nodes/$Elements counts -- see                               !
! WW3_SECOFS/artifacts/mesh_candidates_report.txt "E = 5 m" block; the    !
! 1,305,939 figure quoted when this candidate was chosen was off by 14    !
! nodes from the actual committed .msh file and is superseded here by     !
! the value read directly out of the file).                              !
!                                                                        !
! SUBSEQUENT FIX: a pre-flight validator written against the real WW3    !
! Gmsh reader (model/src/w3triamd.F90 READMSH / model/src/w3servmd.F90   !
! NEXTLN) found the .msh writer (tools/hgrid_to_msh.py) had two fatal    !
! format defects (an interior $Comments block that NEXTLN cannot skip,   !
! and a non-integer 2.2 0 8 MeshFormat version line where READMSH does a !
! list-directed INTEGER read) plus one cosmetic defect (2 boundary node  !
! ids -- the shared corner nodes where open-boundary segments 2 and 3    !
! meet -- listed twice each as separate type-15 elements). All three     !
! were fixed at the writer and every candidate .msh, including           !
! secofs_wave_e5m.msh, was regenerated (node/element counts unchanged;   !
! only the header and the boundary-point element list changed). The de-  !
! duplication drops INBND_COUNT%N_POINT from 1,498 to 1,496 and shrinks  !
! open-boundary segment 3 from 8 to 6 nodes (its first and last nodes    !
! were the same two nodes already listed as segment 2's last and first); !
! the INBND_POINT_NML block below was regenerated from the fixed .msh's  !
! own type-15 elements to match exactly.                                 !
!                                                                        !
! FURTHER SUBSEQUENT FIX (boundary-manifoldness repair): a real WCOSS2   !
! run of ww3_grid on this mesh STOPped inside GET_BOUNDARY               !
! (w3triamd.F90) with "We have a dramatic inconsistency" (IP=276         !
! IP2=274 IP3=273) -- 1,079 boundary nodes had boundary-edge degree 4    !
! (pinch/bowtie points where the elevation-ceiling cut severed a one-    !
! element-wide marsh channel down to a single shared vertex), which no   !
! prior check here had modeled. tools/ww3_boundary_predictor.py          !
! reproduces WW3's own GET_BOUNDARY fan-walk closely enough to predict   !
! that exact IP/IP2/IP3 triple; tools/repair_boundary.py removed the     !
! minority ("neck") triangle wedge at each pinch node (2 rounds, 1,083   !
! triangles), re-applied the connected-component keep rule (drops small  !
! severed fragments with no open-boundary node), and re-derived a        !
! strictly manifold boundary -- confirmed zero inconsistency by the same !
! predictor. secofs_wave_e5m.msh was overwritten in place with the       !
! repaired mesh: now 1,299,007 nodes / 2,505,554 elements (was           !
! 1,305,953 / 2,515,436); secofs_wave_e5m_ESMFmesh.nc/_scrip.nc and      !
! artifacts/secofs_ufs.mesh_wav.nc (regenerated via the real             !
! ESMF_Scrip2Unstruct) were rebuilt from the repaired mesh to match. All !
! 1,496 open-boundary nodes survived unchanged in count/segment grouping !
! (only their ID VALUES shifted from the renumbering); the               !
! INBND_POINT_NML block below reflects the repaired mesh's own node      !
! numbering -- see the block-local note just above it for detail.        !
!                                                                        !
! THIS PASS changed exactly three things relative to the prior DRAFT      !
! (history below); everything else (SPECTRUM_NML, RUN_NML minus one       !
! field, TIMESTEPS_NML, GRID%NAME/TYPE/COORD/CLOS/DMIN, UNST%SF/IDLA/     !
! FORMAT) is untouched and remains exactly as UNVERIFIED/DRAFT as before: !
!                                                                          !
!   1. UNST_NML boundary-index remap (see that section below): the        !
!      INBND_POINT_NML block's 1,498 node ids were re-derived from        !
!      secofs_wave_e5m.msh's OWN filtered/renumbered node numbering       !
!      (previously they were hgrid.gr3's native ids, valid only for the   !
!      unfiltered "samegrid" candidate -- see the CAVEAT that was already !
!      in this file and is now resolved).                                !
!   2. INBND_POINT CONNECT flags changed from "T within a segment, F at   !
!      the last node" to F UNCONDITIONALLY for all 1,498 points -- this   !
!      is a CORRECTNESS FIX, not a style choice: see the INBND_POINT_NML  !
!      section below for the w3gridmd.F90-sourced explanation of why      !
!      CONNECT=T is dangerous for UNST/UNGTYPE grids whose node ids are   !
!      not spatially sequential (true for every candidate here, filtered  !
!      or not).                                                           !
!   3. GRID%ZLIM: corrected/tightened the no-op finding and the value     !
!      (see GRID_NML section below) -- it is now understood to be         !
!      UNCONDITIONALLY inert for GTYPE='UNST', not merely inert when      !
!      UGOBCAUTO=T as previously assumed.                                 !
!   4. (bonus, found during "verify every namelist key" pass) RUN_NML was !
!      missing RUN%FLCK=T -- both real UNST references this file cites    !
!      set it, and the file's own header claimed "all physics terms on"; !
!      added to make the file consistent with its own stated intent.     !
!                                                                          !
! UNST%FILENAME remains the placeholder literal 'secofs_ufs_ww3.msh' --   !
! see MESH_PREP_WCOSS2.md for the instruction to stage/rename the actual  !
! secofs_wave_e5m.msh artifact to that name in the ww3_grid working       !
! directory before running the preprocessor.                              !
!                                                                          !
! ORIGINAL DRAFT PROVENANCE (unchanged from the prior pass, restated for  !
! continuity):                                                            !
!   AK working coupled run has NO ww3_grid.nml -- grid preprocessing was  !
!   already done offline; only the compiled binary mod_def.ww3 is         !
!   present in the Alaska DATM+SCHISM+WW3 reference configuration, no text namelist. AK is   !
!   therefore NOT a syntax source for this file. Namelist syntax verified !
!   against the real vendored WW3 submodule at                            !
!   WW3/regtests/ww3_tp2.6/input/                !
!   ww3_grid_pdlib.nml + namelists_pdlib.nml, and                         !
!   WW3/regtests/ww3_ufs1.1/input_unstr/         !
!   ww3_grid_a.nml + namelists_a.nml. This pass additionally verified     !
!   every namelist key/field name used below directly against            !
!   WW3/model/nml/ww3_grid.nml (the shipped      !
!   documentation template) and the reader code in                       !
!   WW3/model/src/w3gridmd.F90 (SELECT CASE      !
!   (GTYPE) / CASE(UNGTYPE) block, the INBND_POINT_NML consumer loop      !
!   around line 4520-4590, and the ZLIM/TMPSTA classification around      !
!   line 4045-4058) -- not just cross-referenced against other namelist   !
!   examples. Every SECOFS-scale numeric value not explicitly called out  !
!   above (spectral resolution, timesteps) is still a DRAFT and remains   !
!   marked UNVERIFIED at its own namelist block.                          !
!                                                                          !
! COMPANION FILE: this file's GRID%NML points at                          !
!   secofs_ufs_ww3.namelists_pdlib.nml                                    !
! (unchanged by this pass; see DECISIONS.txt).                            !
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------- !

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! SPECTRUM_NML: 36 directions x 32 frequencies, FREQ1=0.0373 Hz,
! XFR=1.1 (geometric frequency progression factor -- 1.1 is the WW3
! default and matches both real references above). NK=32/NTH=36 and
! FREQ1=0.0373 are DRAFT/UNVERIFIED SECOFS decisions (per task
! instruction), not derived from any SECOFS-specific reference -- no
! prior SECOFS wave-spectrum resolution exists to check against. Coarser
! than ww3_ufs1.1's NK=50/NTH=36 (global, deep-water-focused) and finer
! than ww3_tp2.6's NK=25/NTH=24 (small nearshore test); NK=32 sits
! between the two, reasonable for a regional shelf+nearshore domain but
! NOT validated against a resolution/CFL study for the actual SECOFS
! wave mesh.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&SPECTRUM_NML
  SPECTRUM%XFR       = 1.1
  SPECTRUM%FREQ1     = 0.0373
  SPECTRUM%NK        = 32
  SPECTRUM%NTH       = 36
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! RUN_NML: all physics terms on -- matches both real references.
! RUN%FLCK (wavenumber/depth-refraction shift) was MISSING from the prior
! draft (defaults to F if omitted) despite this header already claiming
! "all physics terms on" -- both real UNST references this file cites
! (ww3_tp2.6/ww3_ufs1.1) explicitly set RUN%FLCK=T. Added here to match
! the file's own stated intent; verified field name against
! model/nml/ww3_grid.nml's RUN_NML documentation block (RUN%FLDRY/FLCX/
! FLCY/FLCTH/FLCK/FLSOU, defaults all F). FLDRY (dry run, I/O only) is
! correctly left at its default F -- we want an actual physics run, not
! a dry run -- and was never part of "all propagation/source terms".
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&RUN_NML
  RUN%FLCX         = T
  RUN%FLCY         = T
  RUN%FLCTH        = T
  RUN%FLCK         = T
  RUN%FLSOU        = T
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! TIMESTEPS_NML: DTMAX=DTXY=120s, DTKTH=60s, DTMIN=15s.
!
! Reduced from 360/180 after the first currents-coupled run died with SIGSEGV
! in ST4's tail-stress table. The implicit PDLIB solve was already marginal
! without currents (54 of 60 solves exhausting JGS_MAXITER=1000 with 3-4%
! of nodes unconverged); coupling SCHISM's currents adds the k-shift terms,
! which are built entirely from the current field and its gradients, and tips
! those stalled rows into growth. A smaller propagation step restores diagonal
! dominance and should also clear the chronic iteration stall.
!
! The NUOPC coupling interval stays at 360s -- WW3 substeps internally, so this
! does not change the exchange frequency or the SCHISM dt=120 multiple rule.
!   ww3_grid_a.nml + namelists_a.nml. This pass additionally verified     !
!   every namelist key/field name used below directly against            !
!   WW3/model/nml/ww3_grid.nml (the shipped      !
!   documentation template) and the reader code in                       !
!   WW3/model/src/w3gridmd.F90 (SELECT CASE      !
!   (GTYPE) / CASE(UNGTYPE) block, the INBND_POINT_NML consumer loop      !
!   around line 4520-4590, and the ZLIM/TMPSTA classification around      !
!   line 4045-4058) -- not just cross-referenced against other namelist   !
!   examples. Every SECOFS-scale numeric value not explicitly called out  !
!   above (spectral resolution, timesteps) is still a DRAFT and remains   !
!   marked UNVERIFIED at its own namelist block.                          !
!                                                                          !
! COMPANION FILE: this file's GRID%NML points at                          !
!   secofs_ufs_ww3.namelists_pdlib.nml                                    !
! (unchanged by this pass; see DECISIONS.txt).                            !
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------- !

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! SPECTRUM_NML: 36 directions x 32 frequencies, FREQ1=0.0373 Hz,
! XFR=1.1 (geometric frequency progression factor -- 1.1 is the WW3
! default and matches both real references above). NK=32/NTH=36 and
! FREQ1=0.0373 are DRAFT/UNVERIFIED SECOFS decisions (per task
! instruction), not derived from any SECOFS-specific reference -- no
! prior SECOFS wave-spectrum resolution exists to check against. Coarser
! than ww3_ufs1.1's NK=50/NTH=36 (global, deep-water-focused) and finer
! than ww3_tp2.6's NK=25/NTH=24 (small nearshore test); NK=32 sits
! between the two, reasonable for a regional shelf+nearshore domain but
! NOT validated against a resolution/CFL study for the actual SECOFS
! wave mesh.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&SPECTRUM_NML
  SPECTRUM%XFR       = 1.1
  SPECTRUM%FREQ1     = 0.0373
  SPECTRUM%NK        = 32
  SPECTRUM%NTH       = 36
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! RUN_NML: all physics terms on -- matches both real references.
! RUN%FLCK (wavenumber/depth-refraction shift) was MISSING from the prior
! draft (defaults to F if omitted) despite this header already claiming
! "all physics terms on" -- both real UNST references this file cites
! (ww3_tp2.6/ww3_ufs1.1) explicitly set RUN%FLCK=T. Added here to match
! the file's own stated intent; verified field name against
! model/nml/ww3_grid.nml's RUN_NML documentation block (RUN%FLDRY/FLCX/
! FLCY/FLCTH/FLCK/FLSOU, defaults all F). FLDRY (dry run, I/O only) is
! correctly left at its default F -- we want an actual physics run, not
! a dry run -- and was never part of "all propagation/source terms".
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&RUN_NML
  RUN%FLCX         = T
  RUN%FLCY         = T
  RUN%FLCTH        = T
  RUN%FLCK         = T
  RUN%FLSOU        = T
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! TIMESTEPS_NML: DTMAX=DTXY=360s, DTKTH=180s, DTMIN=15s.
!
! DTMAX/DTXY = 360s is chosen to match the ufs.configure runSeq coupling
! interval (@360, see that file's header) 1:1 -- a clean ratio where one
! WW3 spatial-propagation step spans exactly one coupling window. This
! is a DRAFT choice, reasoned from the coupling-interval divisibility
! constraint (see DECISIONS.txt), not copied from a SECOFS reference.
!
! Real reference values for comparison:
!   ww3_ufs1.1 (global 1deg UNST, explicit PDLIB): DTMAX=720 DTXY=360
!     DTKTH=360 DTMIN=30
!   ww3_tp2.6  (tiny nearshore UNST, implicit PDLIB): all four = 10
!     (dominated by the test's very small/high-res mesh, not comparable
!     in scale)
! DTKTH=180 (half of DTXY) and DTMIN=15 (task-specified "implicit PDLIB"
! class) are UNVERIFIED against SECOFS's actual mesh edge-length/CFL --
! the implicit PDLIB solver (see UNST_NML flags in the companion file)
! is exactly what permits DTXY to exceed the explicit-scheme CFL limit,
! but the real minimum stable value depends on the coarsened wave mesh's
! smallest edge, which does not exist yet (pending mesh tooling). Treat
! DTMAX/DTXY/DTKTH/DTMIN here as a starting point for WCOSS2 CFL
! validation, not a verified stable configuration.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&TIMESTEPS_NML
  TIMESTEPS%DTMAX        = 120.
  TIMESTEPS%DTXY         = 120.
  TIMESTEPS%DTKTH        = 60.
  TIMESTEPS%DTMIN        = 15.
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! GRID_NML: GRID%CLOS='NONE' because SECOFS is a regional (non-global)
! domain -- unlike ww3_ufs1.1's GLOBAL grid (CLOS='T', dateline-closed).
! GRID%COORD='SPHE' (spherical, lon/lat) matches SECOFS's domain bounds
! in secofs_ufs.yaml (grid.domain: lon -88..-63, lat 17..40). GRID%DMIN
! =0.30 matches BOTH real references exactly (a stable WW3-side
! convention for minimum permitted water depth, distinct from SCHISM's
! own min_depth=0.01 in secofs_ufs.yaml -- SCHISM's wetting/drying floor
! is much shallower than WW3's, which is expected: wave physics should
! not be evaluated in near-zero-depth swash/drying zones even where
! SCHISM continues to wet/dry).
!
! GRID%ZLIM -- CORRECTED FINDING (this pass): the prior draft's header
! said ZLIM "only applies when UGOBCAUTO=T" and was a no-op here because
! the companion namelist sets UGOBCAUTO=F. Reading w3gridmd.F90 directly
! shows this is not quite right, and the true situation is simpler and
! more absolute: for GTYPE='UNST' (UNGTYPE), the land/sea classification
! block unconditionally sets TMPSTA=1 for every point --
!   "IF (GTYPE .EQ. UNGTYPE) THEN
!      TMPSTA = 1
!    ELSE
!      DO IY=1, NY
!        DO IX=1, NX
!          IF ( ZBIN(IX,IY) .LE. ZLIM ) TMPSTA(IY,IX) = 1
!        END DO
!      END DO
!    ENDIF"                                   (w3gridmd.F90, ~line 4045)
! i.e. the ZLIM-vs-depth comparison that actually classifies land/sea is
! in the ELSE branch, which UNGTYPE never enters -- every unstructured
! mesh node is treated as a sea point regardless of ZLIM or UGOBCAUTO.
! ZLIM IS still read into a live variable for GTYPE='UNST'
! ("ZLIM = NML_GRID%ZLIM" a few lines earlier in the same CASE(UNGTYPE)
! block), so the namelist field is not rejected/ignored by the parser --
! it is read and then simply never compared against anything for this
! grid type. This makes it UNCONDITIONALLY inert for GTYPE='UNST', not
! merely inert under our specific UGOBCAUTO=F choice as previously
! assumed -- the earlier finding was directionally right (harmless to
! leave as-is) but attributed the no-op to the wrong condition. Value
! changed from the previous placeholder 4.0 to 0.0 (the documented
! namelist default) specifically so that a reader does not infer a
! coastline-depth meaning that the code never applies for this grid
! type; the field is kept (not deleted) because GRID_NML's other
! fields are mandatory and removing a single member of the namelist
! group is not meaningfully different from setting it to the neutral
! default.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&GRID_NML
  GRID%NAME         = 'SECOFS_UFS_WAV'
  GRID%NML          = './secofs_ufs_ww3.namelists_pdlib.nml'
  GRID%TYPE         = 'UNST'
  GRID%COORD        = 'SPHE'
  GRID%CLOS         = 'NONE'
  GRID%ZLIM         = 0.0
  GRID%DMIN         = 0.30
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! UNST_NML: mesh-file block. SF/IDLA/FORMAT match BOTH real references
! exactly (SF=-1 = depths stored as-is/no external scale factor; IDLA=4
! FORMAT='(20f10.2)' is the standard WW3 unstructured .msh read layout)
! -- these three are a stable convention, not a guess. UPDATE (this
! pass): the mesh now exists -- the E=5m elevation-ceiling candidate,
! secofs_wave_e5m.msh (1,305,953 nodes / 2,515,436 elements), was
! selected as final. UNST%FILENAME is kept as the deployment-convention
! literal 'secofs_ufs_ww3.msh' rather than the artifacts/ working name
! 'secofs_wave_e5m.msh' -- MESH_PREP_WCOSS2.md documents staging/
! renaming that file to this literal in the ww3_grid working directory
! before running the preprocessor, so this namelist itself does not
! need to change if a future mesh candidate is ever swapped in.
!
! ALSO VERIFIED (this pass, source-grounded but not itself a change):
! for GTYPE='UNST', w3gridmd.F90 reads depths via a dedicated
! CALL READMSH(NDSG,FNAME) (model/src/w3triamd.F90's READMSH, signature
! (NDS,FNAME) only) rather than through the generic IDLA/IDFM/FORMAT-
! driven array reader (INA2R) used for RECT/CURV DEPTH_NML blocks --
! IDLA and FORMAT are read into local variables but never passed to
! READMSH, so they are not actually consulted while parsing the Gmsh
! file (Gmsh's own file structure is self-describing). UNST%SF (via the
! local variable VSC) IS still applied, directly scaling the depths
! READMSH returns ("ZBIN(:,1) = VSC * ZB(:)"). This matches both real
! UNST regtest examples, which also carry IDLA/FORMAT despite this --
! evidently a stable convention/precedent, not something to change here.
!
! UGOBCAUTO / UGOBCFILE do NOT belong in this UNST_NML block -- they
! live in the auxiliary &UNST group inside the companion GRID%NML file
! (secofs_ufs_ww3.namelists_pdlib.nml), a DIFFERENT namelist despite the
! same name. See that file for the UGOBCAUTO=F / INBND_POINT_NML
! discussion.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&UNST_NML
  UNST%SF          = -1.
  UNST%FILENAME    = 'secofs_wave_e0m_final.msh'
  UNST%IDLA        = 4
  UNST%FORMAT      = '(20f10.2)'
/

! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! INBND_COUNT_NML / INBND_POINT_NML: per-point open-boundary flagging.
! UGOBCAUTO=F in the companion GRID%NML file means WW3 will NOT
! auto-detect boundary nodes from the mesh's own Gmsh type-15 point-
! elements, so every open-boundary node must be listed explicitly
! here (see ALTERNATIVE note below for the auto-detect path that was
! considered and not used).
!
! REMAPPED (this pass) for the FINAL mesh, secofs_wave_e5m.msh (E=5m
! elevation-ceiling candidate). Node ids below are that mesh's OWN
! contiguously-renumbered ids, NOT hgrid.gr3's native ids (those only
! apply to the unfiltered 'samegrid' candidate -- see the CAVEAT that
! used to be here, now resolved).
!
! METHOD: filter_mesh.py's open-boundary remap never drops nodes and
! preserves segment/within-segment order exactly (see that module's
! docstring, step 5, and mesh_candidates_report.txt's "E = 5 m" block:
! "open boundary: 5 segments, 1,498 nodes ... MATCH", "open-boundary
! nodes dropped by filtering: 0"). hgrid_to_msh.py then writes the
! filtered mesh's open-boundary nodes as Gmsh type-15 point elements,
! FIRST in the $Elements block, grouped by segment (geometric-id tag)
! in the SAME preserved order, EXCEPT that a node id already written for
! an earlier segment is not written again (see the SUBSEQUENT FIX note
! at the top of this file) -- 2 of the raw 1,498 NOPE entries are the
! shared corner nodes where segment 2 and segment 3 meet, so the
! de-duplicated .msh carries 1,496 type-15 elements. This pass parsed
! those type-15 lines directly out of the real, committed
! secofs_wave_e5m.msh (the ground-truth artifact itself, not a
! re-derivation of it): 1,496 type-15 entries found, matching the
! de-duplicated count exactly, spanning 5 segments (geometric-id tags
! 1..5) of sizes 1199/281/6/6/4 = 1496.
! Spot-checked node coordinates (read back from the same .msh file's
! $Nodes block) confirm BOTH disjoint shelves are represented and in
! the expected grouping:
!   segment 1 (1199 nodes): SE-US/Gulf shelf, the big ocean-facing arc
!     -- endpoints sampled at (-86.76, 30.39) [Gulf of Mexico, off the
!     FL panhandle] and (-75.31, 37.92) [offshore DE/VA]
!   segment 2 (281 nodes):  Puerto Rico/USVI shelf, the big arc --
!     sampled at (-67.25, 18.48); its last node (-67.31, 18.62) is the
!     corner shared with segment 3's first node
!   segment 3 (6 nodes):    Puerto Rico/USVI shelf, tiny opening near
!     Mona Passage -- sampled at (-67.30, 18.60); its former first/last
!     nodes (duplicates of segment 2's last/first node) are dropped
!   segment 4 (6 nodes):    SE-US/Gulf shelf, tiny river-mouth opening
!     near Savannah, GA -- sampled at (-81.27, 32.53)
!   segment 5 (4 nodes):    SE-US/Gulf shelf, tiny river-mouth opening
!     near Winyah Bay, SC -- sampled at (-79.97, 33.21)
! i.e. segments {1,4,5} = 1199+6+4 = 1,209 nodes are the SE-US/Gulf
! shelf and segments {2,3} = 281+6 = 287 nodes are the Puerto Rico/USVI
! shelf -- the 1,209/287 split differs from the 1,209/289 figure
! documented since the original (pre-mesh-selection) draft of this file
! only by the 2 de-duplicated corner nodes (both counted, before this
! fix, on the Puerto Rico/USVI side since that is where segment 3
! lived) -- both disjoint shelves are present and correctly represented
! in the final, mesh-specific node numbering.
!
! Fields are X_INDEX, Y_INDEX, CONNECT (model/src/w3gridmd.F90's
! NML_INBND_POINT_T / READ_INBOUND_NML, confirmed directly against the
! type declaration at w3nmlgridmd.F90 ~line 200-204 and the consumer
! loop at w3gridmd.F90 ~line 4520-4590, not just the tp2.6 example's
! bare numbers). X_INDEX=node id (1-based, this mesh's own numbering).
! Y_INDEX=1 always: for a structured grid this would be the second (IY)
! grid index, but an unstructured (UNGTYPE) mesh has no second
! dimension, so WW3's own bounds check just requires 1<=Y_INDEX<=NY
! with NY=1 for UNGTYPE -- 1 is the only legal value.
!
! CONNECT -- CHANGED to F for EVERY point (this pass; CORRECTNESS FIX,
! not a style choice). The prior draft set CONNECT=T within a segment
! (F only at each segment's last node), on the stated theory that T
! means "this node's spectrum interpolates continuously with the next
! node in the same NOPE segment". Reading w3gridmd.F90's actual consumer
! loop shows that theory does not describe what CONNECT does, and that
! using T here would have been actively harmful:
!   IF ( CONNCT .AND. .NOT.FIRST ) THEN
!     IDX = IX - IXO ;  IDY = IY - IYO
!     IF ( IDX.EQ.0 .OR. IDY.EQ.0 .OR. ABS(IDX).EQ.ABS(IDY) ) THEN
!       NBA = MAX( MAX(ABS(IDX),ABS(IDY)) - 1, 0 )
!       ... loop IBA=1,NBA stepping IX by SIGN(IDX) each time,
!           promoting TMPSTA(IY,IX) to boundary status if it is
!           currently a sea point ...
!                                          (w3gridmd.F90, ~line 4558-4580)
! CONNECT=T triggers a "fill in the intermediate grid-line points"
! behaviour meant for STRUCTURED (RECT/CURV) grids, where IX/IY are 2-D
! grid indices and an intermediate index genuinely sits geographically
! between two boundary points. For UNGTYPE, Y_INDEX is pinned at 1, so
! IDY is always 0, which makes the guard condition
! "IDX.EQ.0 .OR. IDY.EQ.0 .OR. ABS(IDX).EQ.ABS(IDY)" ALWAYS true --
! the fill-in branch always activates whenever CONNECT=T. NBA then
! becomes ABS(IDX)-1, i.e. the raw difference between two consecutive
! listed nodes' 1-based mesh-wide ids minus one, and every INTEGER id in
! that range gets walked and (since GTYPE='UNST' unconditionally sets
! TMPSTA=1 for every node, see the GRID_NML/ZLIM note above) promoted to
! active-boundary status if currently 1 -- which is every node. Because
! this mesh's contiguous renumbering scatters along-boundary-adjacent
! nodes across the FULL 1..1,305,953 id range (consecutive boundary
! nodes here are commonly ~1,500-2,500 ids apart, and segment 1 alone
! spans ids 26,674 to 992,584), CONNECT=T would have caused ww3_grid to
! silently promote a huge, essentially arbitrary swath of interior mesh
! nodes to "active open-boundary point" status -- nodes with no real
! spectral forcing, an error rather than a convenience. This is a
! structured-grid feature that does not have a safe meaning for an
! unstructured mesh's arbitrary node numbering; it was already latent
! in the prior draft (which used hgrid.gr3's native ids -- also not
! spatially sequential along the boundary), not something newly
! introduced by the renumbering itself. Setting CONNECT=F for every
! entry is safe and sufficient: every one of the 1,496 real
! open-boundary nodes is already listed explicitly and individually, so
! no "fill between" convenience is needed. (UGOBCAUTO=T, bypassing this
! whole INBND_POINT_NML mechanism in favour of the mesh's own type-15
! elements, remains a simpler and structurally safer alternative worth
! considering separately -- not adopted here since it would mean editing
! the companion namelist, out of this pass's scope.)
!
! ALTERNATIVE NOTED, NOT USED: w3gridmd.F90 (~line 4550) skips this
! entire INBND_POINT_NML list for UNGTYPE grids whenever UGOBCAUTO or
! UGOBCOK is TRUE ('IF (GTYPE.EQ.UNGTYPE.AND.(UGOBCAUTO.OR.UGOBCOK))
! CYCLE') -- i.e. WW3 can auto-detect every boundary node directly from
! the mesh's own Gmsh type-15 point-elements instead of an explicit
! list. This repo's companion namelist already commits to UGOBCAUTO=F
! (explicit list), matching the tp2.6 reference -- this block is the
! fill-in for that choice, not a proposal to change it.
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
! REMAPPED AGAIN (boundary-repair pass): secofs_wave_e5m.msh was found  !
! to fail ww3_grid at runtime (GET_BOUNDARY, w3triamd.F90 -- "We have a !
! dramatic inconsistency", IP=276 IP2=274 IP3=273) because 1,079 boundary!
! nodes had boundary-edge degree 4 (pinch/bowtie points where the       !
! elevation-ceiling cut severed a one-element-wide marsh channel down   !
! to a single shared vertex -- see tools/ww3_boundary_predictor.py and  !
! tools/repair_boundary.py). tools/repair_boundary.py removed the       !
! minority ("neck") triangle wedge at each pinch node, iterated to a    !
! fixed point (2 rounds, 1,083 triangles removed directly) and then     !
! re-applied the connected-component keep rule (drops small severed     !
! fragments with no open-boundary node), yielding a strictly manifold   !
! boundary confirmed by the GET_BOUNDARY predictor: 1,299,007 nodes /    !
! 2,505,554 elements (was 1,305,953 / 2,515,436), all renumbered         !
! contiguously 1..1,299,007. secofs_wave_e5m.msh was overwritten in      !
! place with this repaired mesh. ALL 1,496 open-boundary nodes survived  !
! repair unchanged in count and segment grouping (segment sizes [1199, 281, 6, 6, 4]!
! matches the pre-repair block exactly), but their ID VALUES shifted     !
! because of the contiguous renumbering, so the INBND_POINT_NML block    !
! below was regenerated from the repaired .msh's own type-15 elements    !
! (ground truth, not re-derived indirectly). CONNECT remains F           !
! unconditionally for every point -- see the CONNECT correctness-fix     !
! discussion above this block, unaffected by the repair.                 !
! -------------------------------------------------------------------- !
&INBND_COUNT_NML
  INBND_COUNT%N_POINT = 1496
/

&INBND_POINT_NML
  INBND_POINT(1) = 7148 1 F
  INBND_POINT(2) = 7598 1 F
